; expect 42
; The same rule for the OTHER way a name fails to be exported: the
; declaration is `pub`, and the import that brought the module in does
; not name it.
;
; `(import PrivSel (viaSibling))` asks for one of two public
; functions. `viaSibling` calls `sibling`, which the name list does
; not mention - so under the old rule `sibling` was deleted and this
; file did not compile, and if this file happened to define `sibling`
; itself, `viaSibling` called THAT instead, silently, at exit 0.
;
;   (viaSibling 2)  18 - the module's own `sibling`, twice. The
;                        importer's `sibling` below would give 8.
;   (sibling 2)      4 - this file's own definition, unmoved. It is
;                        not the module's: the name list is what
;                        decides, and it does not carry `sibling`.
(import PrivSel (viaSibling))

(:: sibling (-> Int Int))

(fn (sibling n) (+ n 2))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (if (== (+ (viaSibling 2) (sibling 2)) 22)
    42
    1
  )
)
